{"ukmt-tmc-2025-regional-crossnumber-01":[{"title":"Solution","body":"Start with the clues that stand alone.\n\n- 6A $=45$; 13D $=55$; 14A $=2\\times6\\times9=162$; 17D $=11\\times10\\times9=990$; 3A: $(990-900)=90$ sides, angle $180-360/90=176$.\n- 7A: $3\\times$ (palindrome 9D). 9D $=222$ gives $666$. 24D $=162-45=117$. 28A: Fibonacci number $377$; its largest two-digit factor is $29$ ($377=13\\times29$), so 4D is a multiple of 29: $667=23\\times29$.\n- 25A: smallest two-digit factors: $11$ of $55$ and $13$ of $117$, product $143$. 26A: $990\\div55=18$, so 5D: interior angle of an 18-gon $=160$. 22D: rearrangement of 143: $314$.\n- 16A: $n^2+\\frac54n=159$ for $n=12$. 2D $=(143+159)/2=151$, and 3D $=151$ (odd).\n- 15D: $n^2+\\frac n2=68$ for $n=8$; 12A: $68^2=4624$, so $24$. 16D $=144$, 21A: $13\\times41$ gives 21D $=533$ (23A $=410=2\\cdot5\\cdot41$); 23A: $\\frac{31}{66}\\times990=465$, $465-55=410$. 21A is the prime $523$.\n- 20D: the polygon with more than 25 sides: the 30-gon has interior angle $168$, and $168-55=113$. 8A $=112$. 1A $=(113+117)/2=115$. 10A: its clue is the mean of 10A and 24D; this forces 10A to equal 24D, $117$. 18D $=(112/56)^5=32$; 19A $=55-1=54$; 11D $=\\frac12(1+6+2+1+5+9)=12$; 27A $=32\\times12=384$; 8D $=121$\n\nCompleted answers: Across: 1: 115, 3: 176, 6: 45, 7: 666, 8: 112, 10: 117, 12: 24, 14: 162, 16: 159, 19: 54, 21: 523, 23: 410, 25: 143, 26: 18, 27: 384, 28: 377. Down: 2: 151, 3: 151, 4: 667, 5: 160, 8: 121, 9: 222, 11: 12, 13: 55, 15: 68, 16: 144, 17: 990, 18: 32, 20: 113, 21: 533, 22: 314, 24: 117.\n\n**Answer: the completed grid above.**"}]}